1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,439
On November 12th, 2026

2
00:00:02,439 --> 00:00:04,361
Nvidia filed a surprise

3
00:00:04,361 --> 00:00:06,284
notice with regulators.

4
00:00:06,634 --> 00:00:09,429
Jensen Huang, who co-founded the company

5
00:00:09,429 --> 00:00:11,666
was out as CEO and as a director

6
00:00:11,666 --> 00:00:13,203
effective immediately.

7
00:00:13,553 --> 00:00:15,608
He'd run Nvidia for 33 years

8
00:00:15,608 --> 00:00:17,150
and the filing didn't

9
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:18,838
say why he was leaving.

10
00:00:19,188 --> 00:00:20,357
The stock opened down

11
00:00:20,357 --> 00:00:21,526
more than ten percent

12
00:00:21,526 --> 00:00:23,586
and still closed down more than four.

13
00:00:23,936 --> 00:00:25,886
Four days before the filing

14
00:00:25,886 --> 00:00:28,052
he'd written to all his staff.

15
00:00:28,052 --> 00:00:29,063
One line read:

16
00:00:29,063 --> 00:00:31,229
"I was unfaithful to my wife."

17
00:00:31,579 --> 00:00:33,176
He didn't say with whom.

18
00:00:33,176 --> 00:00:35,306
It looked like a private matter.

19
00:00:35,656 --> 00:00:38,374
And yet within days, Nvidia's board

20
00:00:38,374 --> 00:00:39,538
the US Congress

21
00:00:39,538 --> 00:00:41,712
and China's Foreign Ministry

22
00:00:41,712 --> 00:00:43,343
were all weighing in.

23
00:00:43,693 --> 00:00:44,837
So here's our take:

24
00:00:44,837 --> 00:00:47,126
the US-China tech war turned one CEO's

25
00:00:47,126 --> 00:00:49,776
private life into a national security issue.

26
00:00:50,126 --> 00:00:51,661
That's not the same as saying

27
00:00:51,661 --> 00:00:53,302
he quit over national security.

28
00:00:53,302 --> 00:00:55,526
The company still hasn't said why he left.

29
00:01:04,333 --> 00:01:05,141
Before we go on

30
00:01:05,141 --> 00:01:06,810
let's separate what's confirmed

31
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:08,480
from what's only been reported.

32
00:01:08,830 --> 00:01:10,628
Here's what's on the record.

33
00:01:10,628 --> 00:01:12,297
Huang admitted, in writing

34
00:01:12,297 --> 00:01:13,774
that he was unfaithful.

35
00:01:14,124 --> 00:01:16,273
Nvidia's board opened an investigation

36
00:01:16,273 --> 00:01:17,800
citing reports that foreign

37
00:01:17,800 --> 00:01:19,497
intelligence had targeted him.

38
00:01:19,847 --> 00:01:21,627
But the board also said it had no

39
00:01:21,627 --> 00:01:23,515
reason to believe national security

40
00:01:23,515 --> 00:01:25,672
or company secrets had been compromised.

41
00:01:26,022 --> 00:01:27,222
And then he resigned

42
00:01:27,222 --> 00:01:28,482
with no reason given.

43
00:01:28,832 --> 00:01:30,316
Now, two more claims.

44
00:01:30,316 --> 00:01:32,505
Both are New York Times reports

45
00:01:32,505 --> 00:01:34,201
citing anonymous sources

46
00:01:34,201 --> 00:01:35,967
and neither is confirmed.

47
00:01:36,317 --> 00:01:38,602
One says US intelligence suspected

48
00:01:38,602 --> 00:01:41,895
a businesswoman close to him of spying for China.

49
00:01:42,245 --> 00:01:43,866
She's a former executive

50
00:01:43,866 --> 00:01:45,351
at a Singapore company

51
00:01:45,351 --> 00:01:47,985
and that company is an Nvidia customer.

52
00:01:48,335 --> 00:01:50,948
The other says he'd had another relationship

53
00:01:50,948 --> 00:01:53,205
with an executive who reported to him.

54
00:01:53,205 --> 00:01:54,808
Nvidia declined to comment.

55
00:01:55,158 --> 00:01:56,468
Now, the other side.

56
00:01:56,468 --> 00:01:58,433
China's Foreign Ministry calls

57
00:01:58,433 --> 00:02:01,249
the "Chinese spy" claims groundless smears.

58
00:02:01,599 --> 00:02:03,195
Singapore's Ministry of Home

59
00:02:03,195 --> 00:02:05,134
Affairs says it never investigated

60
00:02:05,134 --> 00:02:06,958
the businesswoman for espionage.

61
00:02:07,308 --> 00:02:08,487
Singapore police did

62
00:02:08,487 --> 00:02:10,374
look into her personally, though

63
00:02:10,374 --> 00:02:12,319
over suspected false declarations

64
00:02:12,319 --> 00:02:14,206
when the company bought servers.

65
00:02:14,556 --> 00:02:16,578
This February, they said there wasn't

66
00:02:16,578 --> 00:02:18,109
enough evidence against her.

67
00:02:18,109 --> 00:02:19,639
And no one has been charged.

68
00:02:19,989 --> 00:02:21,842
So was the affair in his letter

69
00:02:21,842 --> 00:02:23,217
with the businesswoman?

70
00:02:23,217 --> 00:02:25,189
He didn't say, and we don't know.

71
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:31,846
Either way, here's the real puzzle:

72
00:02:31,846 --> 00:02:33,716
why would someone close to a CEO

73
00:02:33,716 --> 00:02:35,177
set off this many alarms?

74
00:02:35,527 --> 00:02:37,382
Because Nvidia sells AI chips

75
00:02:37,382 --> 00:02:40,004
and whether the best ones can go to China

76
00:02:40,004 --> 00:02:42,178
is the front line of the tech war.

77
00:02:42,528 --> 00:02:44,166
Nvidia's own annual report

78
00:02:44,166 --> 00:02:46,055
admits it's basically shut out

79
00:02:46,055 --> 00:02:47,945
of China's data center market.

80
00:02:48,295 --> 00:02:50,596
And what Washington fears most is chips

81
00:02:50,596 --> 00:02:53,133
slipping into China through Southeast Asia.

82
00:02:53,483 --> 00:02:54,866
That's where the Singapore

83
00:02:54,866 --> 00:02:55,770
company comes in.

84
00:02:55,770 --> 00:02:57,632
The evidence against her fell short

85
00:02:57,632 --> 00:02:58,696
but police are still

86
00:02:58,696 --> 00:03:00,451
investigating the company itself.

87
00:03:00,801 --> 00:03:02,595
US export control officials

88
00:03:02,595 --> 00:03:04,056
have inspected it too.

89
00:03:04,056 --> 00:03:06,248
The company denies any wrongdoing

90
00:03:06,248 --> 00:03:08,241
and there's no conclusion yet.

91
00:03:08,591 --> 00:03:10,132
So you've got the top chips

92
00:03:10,132 --> 00:03:12,300
a customer suspected of rerouting them

93
00:03:12,300 --> 00:03:13,955
and someone close to the CEO.

94
00:03:14,305 --> 00:03:15,570
Put those three together

95
00:03:15,570 --> 00:03:17,363
and a private matter ends up under

96
00:03:17,363 --> 00:03:19,314
a national security magnifying glass.

97
00:03:19,664 --> 00:03:21,675
Now look at how everyone responded.

98
00:03:21,675 --> 00:03:23,686
They all pointed to the same thing.

99
00:03:24,036 --> 00:03:25,901
The board cited reports that foreign

100
00:03:25,901 --> 00:03:27,456
intelligence had targeted him.

101
00:03:27,806 --> 00:03:29,722
Three Republican committee chairmen

102
00:03:29,722 --> 00:03:31,693
asked him for a closed-door briefing

103
00:03:31,693 --> 00:03:33,281
on national security grounds.

104
00:03:33,631 --> 00:03:34,879
On the day he resigned

105
00:03:34,879 --> 00:03:36,410
the head of the House China

106
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:38,396
committee said Nvidia needs leaders

107
00:03:38,396 --> 00:03:40,211
who put national security first.

108
00:03:40,561 --> 00:03:42,872
And that chairman urged Congress

109
00:03:42,872 --> 00:03:45,472
to move fast on export control laws.

110
00:03:45,822 --> 00:03:47,070
Even China's Foreign

111
00:03:47,070 --> 00:03:48,755
Ministry made it about tech

112
00:03:48,755 --> 00:03:51,314
telling the US to stop fabricating a "spy

113
00:03:51,314 --> 00:03:53,685
narrative" to justify a tech blockade.

114
00:03:54,035 --> 00:03:55,618
So for all the shouting

115
00:03:55,618 --> 00:03:57,683
both sides agree on one thing:

116
00:03:57,683 --> 00:03:59,059
this is about chips.

117
00:04:03,667 --> 00:04:05,121
Now, inside Washington.

118
00:04:05,121 --> 00:04:06,511
On chip sales to China

119
00:04:06,511 --> 00:04:09,103
lawmakers in both parties want to tighten

120
00:04:09,103 --> 00:04:11,505
while the White House keeps loosening.

121
00:04:11,855 --> 00:04:14,370
And Huang was on the White House's side

122
00:04:14,370 --> 00:04:15,852
so when the scandal hit

123
00:04:15,852 --> 00:04:18,367
both parties used it to demand answers.

124
00:04:18,717 --> 00:04:20,956
Take the tightening side first.

125
00:04:20,956 --> 00:04:22,039
In October 2025

126
00:04:22,039 --> 00:04:25,000
the Senate voted 77 to 20 for a provision

127
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:27,095
limiting chip sales to China.

128
00:04:27,445 --> 00:04:28,628
Then in March 2026

129
00:04:28,628 --> 00:04:30,929
one senator from each party jointly

130
00:04:30,929 --> 00:04:32,835
asked the Commerce Department

131
00:04:32,835 --> 00:04:35,135
to review Nvidia's export licenses.

132
00:04:35,485 --> 00:04:37,481
Trump went the other way.

133
00:04:37,481 --> 00:04:38,599
In August 2025

134
00:04:38,599 --> 00:04:41,632
he said he'd clear a chip for China if

135
00:04:41,632 --> 00:04:45,225
Nvidia would "pay us as a country something."

136
00:04:45,575 --> 00:04:47,411
And after the scandal broke

137
00:04:47,411 --> 00:04:50,063
he called Huang "a very honorable man."

138
00:04:50,413 --> 00:04:51,852
Democrats went after Trump

139
00:04:51,852 --> 00:04:53,180
asking whether officials

140
00:04:53,180 --> 00:04:54,729
were told not to investigate

141
00:04:54,729 --> 00:04:56,555
because of Trump's ties to Huang.

142
00:04:56,905 --> 00:04:58,971
Republicans went after the Commerce

143
00:04:58,971 --> 00:05:01,510
Department and pushed export control bills.

144
00:05:01,860 --> 00:05:04,193
But don't draw that line too neatly.

145
00:05:04,193 --> 00:05:05,878
Senator Warren, a Democrat

146
00:05:05,878 --> 00:05:07,823
is also backing a Republican's

147
00:05:07,823 --> 00:05:09,378
bill to tighten exports.

148
00:05:09,728 --> 00:05:11,913
The one clean difference is this:

149
00:05:11,913 --> 00:05:14,561
Democrats name Trump. Republicans don't.

150
00:05:19,167 --> 00:05:20,832
Speaking of the two parties

151
00:05:20,832 --> 00:05:22,188
there's a popular take

152
00:05:22,188 --> 00:05:23,545
in Chinese commentary:

153
00:05:23,545 --> 00:05:25,086
that Huang was a casualty

154
00:05:25,086 --> 00:05:26,813
of America's partisan fight.

155
00:05:27,163 --> 00:05:29,525
Hong Kong outlet HK01 says Hu Xijin

156
00:05:29,525 --> 00:05:31,685
a well-known Chinese commentator

157
00:05:31,685 --> 00:05:33,237
first claimed Democrats

158
00:05:33,237 --> 00:05:35,397
and the Washington establishment

159
00:05:35,397 --> 00:05:37,894
were targeting Huang to get at Trump.

160
00:05:38,244 --> 00:05:40,253
A day later, he changed his tune:

161
00:05:40,253 --> 00:05:42,080
Huang was a political casualty

162
00:05:42,080 --> 00:05:43,906
of vicious two-party fighting.

163
00:05:44,256 --> 00:05:46,988
So first he blamed the Democrats

164
00:05:46,988 --> 00:05:48,525
then both parties.

165
00:05:48,875 --> 00:05:49,779
So we checked.

166
00:05:49,779 --> 00:05:50,942
Before he resigned

167
00:05:50,942 --> 00:05:52,621
the only lawmaker we found

168
00:05:52,621 --> 00:05:54,559
publicly calling for him to go

169
00:05:54,559 --> 00:05:56,884
was Republican Senator Kevin Cramer.

170
00:05:57,234 --> 00:05:59,452
And Trump, a Republican president

171
00:05:59,452 --> 00:06:01,132
backed him the whole way.

172
00:06:01,482 --> 00:06:03,340
The top House Democrat did say

173
00:06:03,340 --> 00:06:05,693
resigning "was the right thing to do,"

174
00:06:05,693 --> 00:06:07,984
but only after he'd already resigned.

175
00:06:08,334 --> 00:06:09,737
There's no record of either

176
00:06:09,737 --> 00:06:10,881
party pushing him out.

177
00:06:11,231 --> 00:06:12,915
Besides, the board had plenty

178
00:06:12,915 --> 00:06:15,005
of reasons of its own: his admission

179
00:06:15,005 --> 00:06:16,979
the report of another relationship

180
00:06:16,979 --> 00:06:18,488
and a shareholder lawsuit.

181
00:06:18,838 --> 00:06:21,275
We'd say no one sacrificed him on purpose.

182
00:06:21,275 --> 00:06:23,654
He did get caught in other people's fight

183
00:06:23,654 --> 00:06:25,801
but nothing shows that's why he left.

184
00:06:30,433 --> 00:06:31,940
Not everyone buys this.

185
00:06:31,940 --> 00:06:33,710
The strongest pushback says

186
00:06:33,710 --> 00:06:35,741
national security is just noise

187
00:06:35,741 --> 00:06:38,625
and he left over plain corporate governance.

188
00:06:38,975 --> 00:06:40,205
He admitted infidelity

189
00:06:40,205 --> 00:06:41,268
then a relationship

190
00:06:41,268 --> 00:06:43,001
with a subordinate was reported

191
00:06:43,001 --> 00:06:44,231
and the board accepted

192
00:06:44,231 --> 00:06:45,909
his resignation that very day.

193
00:06:46,259 --> 00:06:48,914
Any CEO could be gone over that. In 2018

194
00:06:48,914 --> 00:06:51,568
Intel's CEO resigned over a relationship

195
00:06:51,568 --> 00:06:54,422
with an employee that broke company policy.

196
00:06:54,772 --> 00:06:56,143
We agree with all of that

197
00:06:56,143 --> 00:06:57,514
and we never said he left

198
00:06:57,514 --> 00:06:58,776
over national security.

199
00:06:58,776 --> 00:07:00,970
The direct cause may well be governance.

200
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:03,452
But we're asking a different question:

201
00:07:03,452 --> 00:07:05,752
how does one CEO's affair end up in front

202
00:07:05,752 --> 00:07:08,052
of Congress and China's Foreign Ministry?

203
00:07:08,402 --> 00:07:09,953
Intel's case never did.

204
00:07:09,953 --> 00:07:11,774
And we think the difference

205
00:07:11,774 --> 00:07:12,988
isn't what he did.

206
00:07:12,988 --> 00:07:14,877
It's what his company sells.

207
00:07:15,227 --> 00:07:16,508
Even if the spy reports

208
00:07:16,508 --> 00:07:17,622
turn out to be false

209
00:07:17,622 --> 00:07:19,126
everyone's already treating

210
00:07:19,126 --> 00:07:21,020
this as a national security issue.

211
00:07:25,733 --> 00:07:27,841
When the headlines fade, remember this:

212
00:07:27,841 --> 00:07:29,841
the more critical a company's product

213
00:07:29,841 --> 00:07:32,003
the less private its leader's life gets.

214
00:07:32,353 --> 00:07:33,613
Where his private life

215
00:07:33,613 --> 00:07:34,816
ends isn't up to him.

216
00:07:34,816 --> 00:07:36,763
What his company sells decides it.

217
00:07:37,113 --> 00:07:38,796
Coming up on November 17th

218
00:07:38,796 --> 00:07:40,350
Nvidia reports its first

219
00:07:40,350 --> 00:07:42,293
earnings without Jensen Huang.

220
00:07:42,643 --> 00:07:44,050
There's no date yet for him

221
00:07:44,050 --> 00:07:45,144
to go before Congress

222
00:07:45,144 --> 00:07:46,707
and none of the investigations

223
00:07:46,707 --> 00:07:47,489
has wrapped up.

224
00:07:47,839 --> 00:07:49,269
That's where things stand

225
00:07:49,269 --> 00:07:50,414
as of November 14th.

226
00:07:50,414 --> 00:07:52,302
None of this is investment advice

227
00:07:52,302 --> 00:07:54,304
and we'll keep following the story.
